<DocsLayout title="Packages and applications" subtitle="Workspace names, public entry points, consumers, and implementation responsibilities." description="Workspace names, public entry points, consumers, and implementation responsibilities." toc={toc}>
  <section id="domain" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Domain libraries</h2>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/core</code></h3>
    <p><code>packages/core/src/index.ts</code> exports CAD symbols both flat and under <code>cad</code>, CAM under <code>cam</code>, and drawing functions under <code>drawing</code>. It contains sketches, solid modelling, tessellation, drawing exchange, machining operations, post-processors, G-code analysis, and verification. The desktop consumes this package directly for interactive operations; the sidecar uses it behind HTTP and assistant adapters.</p>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/vcs</code></h3>
    <p><code>packages/vcs/src/index.ts</code> exposes project snapshot, object, reference, diff, merge, and GitHub transport functions. It has no runtime package dependencies. Storage and acting-user resolution belong to the sidecar's VCS repository and services.</p>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/workflow</code></h3>
    <p><code>packages/workflow/src/index.ts</code> exports workflow schemas, parsing, trigger matching, and execution. It depends on Effect. The sidecar registers steps requiring notifications, project history, or AI access; a workflow step name is usable only when its implementation is registered in the runner.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="backend" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Contracts, storage, and AI</h2>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/api</code></h3>
    <p>The root entry <code>packages/api/src/index.ts</code> composes HttpApi groups and shared schemas. The <code>@cadcamfun/api/sdk</code> export points to the generated Promise SDK. The source tree also contains Effect clients and the full contract-based <code>ApiClient</code>. API consumers should select the client surface that represents the endpoint they need.</p>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/db</code></h3>
    <p>The root export provides the Effect <code>Database</code> service and a schema namespace. <code>@cadcamfun/db/schema</code> exposes the Drizzle tables. SQLite access, migrations, backup planning, and schema ownership live here. Most application queries run from sidecar repositories through the service; the local web Studio imports table schemas for its independent Bun process.</p>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/ai</code></h3>
    <p><code>packages/ai/src/index.ts</code> exposes provider services, the embedded nikcli integration, action catalogue, application tools, prompts, and skills. The sidecar supplies live project, geometry, machining, file, history, and workflow adapters. The package depends on API and core, with pinned nikcli and AI SDK versions.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Extension packages</h2>
    <h3><code>@cadcamfun/plugin-sdk</code></h3>
    <p>The SDK's <code>src/index.ts</code> is the public application plugin entry point. It defines manifest and capability contracts and plugin authoring helpers. The runtime host lives in <code>apps/sidecar/src/plugins</code>; the SDK itself is not the host process.</p>
    <h3><code>@nikoemme_/cadcamfun-nikcli-plugin</code></h3>
    <p><code>packages/nikcli-plugin/src/index.ts</code> exports the standalone nikcli bridge. Unlike most workspaces, it is configured for public npm publication, currently at version <code>0.1.0</code>. It discovers actions from a running CADCAMFUN application and invokes them over authenticated HTTP. It does not embed the CAD kernel or an independent action catalogue.</p>
    <p><code>@cadcamfun/plugin-sdk</code> implements plugin API major version <code>1</code>. Plugin identifiers are reverse-DNS names of 3–128 characters, such as <code>com.example.gear-generator</code>, and versions follow <code>major.minor.patch</code> with an optional pre-release suffix (<code>packages/plugin-sdk/src/manifest.ts</code>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="codegen" class="doc-section">
    <h2>HTTP client generator</h2>
    <p><code>@cadcamfun/httpapi-codegen</code> exports its compiler and emitters from <code>packages/httpapi-codegen/src/index.ts</code>. <code>compile</code> reflects an Effect HttpApi into a contract model. <code>emitPromise</code> creates the Promise SDK; <code>emitEffect</code> and <code>emitEffectImported</code> support Effect output. The imported variant references existing contract schemas.</p>
    <p>Other exports include compatibility emitters, transport error mapping, <code>write</code>, and <code>generate</code>. Unsupported contract shapes raise <code>GenerationError</code>. The application generation script records endpoint exclusions explicitly for shared and Promise-only limitations.</p>
    <p><code>packages/api/scripts/generate-clients.ts</code> writes <code>src/sdk/generated</code> and <code>src/client/generated</code>. Its drift check generates into a temporary directory and compares file names and contents with committed output.</p>    <DocCode language="bash" title="Repository root">{`bun run generate
bun run generate:check`}</DocCode>
    <p>The generated output lives in <code>packages/api/src/client/generated</code> and <code>packages/api/src/sdk/generated</code>. Run <code>bun run generate</code> after changing a contract, and <code>bun run generate:check</code> to confirm the committed output is current.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="apps" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Application workspaces</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt><code>@cadcamfun/sidecar</code> — <code>apps/sidecar</code></dt><dd><code>src/main.ts</code> is the executable server entry; <code>src/layers.ts</code> wires services and served groups. The package start script runs Bun directly. Compilation into a native sidecar is managed by root build scripts.</dd>
      <dt><code>@cadcamfun/desktop</code> — <code>apps/desktop</code></dt><dd>SolidJS/Vite frontend plus the Tauri Rust application in <code>src-tauri</code>. The package's <code>build</code> script builds the frontend; native packaging requires Tauri and the sidecar resources.</dd>
      <dt><code>@cadcamfun/mobile</code> — <code>apps/mobile</code></dt><dd>Expo Router entry point, companion HTTP/native transport, secure pairing state, and mobile UI. Its build script exports the iOS JavaScript bundle; producing a native application requires the native build commands.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="web" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Documentation, assistant, and Studio</h2>
    <p><code>@cadcamfun/web</code> contains Astro pages, shared documentation components, and the site content index. The sidebar is declared in <code>src/data/docsSidebar.ts</code>; <code>src/data/docsIndex.ts</code> holds retrieval summaries and keywords for the same routes. Each documentation page prerenders its article.</p>
    <p><code>GET /llm</code> lists documentation pages as Markdown links. <code>GET /llm?path=/docs/cam</code> returns the corresponding rendered article as GitHub-flavored Markdown. Add <code>format=prompt</code> for a documentation prompt. Unknown paths return 404; conversion failures return an error status. The Markdown pipeline strips navigation, scripts, styles, and controls.</p>
    <p><code>POST /api/docs-assistant</code> retrieves up to three indexed pages and streams an answer through Workers AI or a configured nikcli backend. Local Astro development disables remote bindings, so a model backend must be available for generation. The optional <code>SESSIONS</code> KV binding enables best-effort per-IP rate limiting; optional <code>ARTIFACTS</code> storage enables shareable generated guides.</p>
    <p><code>bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web dev</code> starts both Astro and the Studio API. <code>start</code> runs Astro alone; <code>studio</code> starts only the Bun Studio API. Studio selects <code>CADCAMFUN_DB</code>, then <code>DATABASE_URL</code> with an optional <code>file:</code> prefix removed, then the repository's <code>data/cadcamfun.db</code>. The file must already exist and contain the application schema.</p>
    <p>Studio accepts an existing user's email and password, verifies the stored password with <code>Bun.password</code>, and writes a seven-day database session with an HTTP-only cookie. Its overview returns installation-wide counts and recent projects to authenticated users. The production <code>/studio/api/*</code> route returns 503; deployed documentation does not open a local SQLite database.</p>    <DocCode language="bash" title="Repository root">{`bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web start
bun test packages/web/test
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web typecheck
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web build`}</DocCode>
  </section>


  <section id="select-an-api" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Select an integration surface</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Surface</th><th>What the caller provides</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Compute geometry without persistence</td><td><code>@cadcamfun/core</code></td><td>Validated geometry and operation inputs; ownership and document saving remain outside the kernel.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call the complete typed HTTP contract</td><td><code>ApiClient</code> from <code>@cadcamfun/api</code></td><td>Sidecar base URL, application token, and Effect runtime/layers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Use Promise-based generated methods</td><td><code>@cadcamfun/api/sdk</code></td><td>SDK client options and endpoint inputs; check generation omissions first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Offer an application capability</td><td><code>@cadcamfun/plugin-sdk</code></td><td>A supported manifest, compatible module exports, and granted capabilities.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drive project actions from standalone nikcli</td><td><code>@nikoemme_/cadcamfun-nikcli-plugin</code></td><td>The running app URL, scoped bridge token, and selected project.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Read documentation in a tool</td><td><code>/llm?path=/docs/…</code></td><td>A known documentation route; this does not invoke CAD/CAM project actions.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Most library packages are private workspaces exporting TypeScript source; their presence in the monorepo does not imply a separately published npm distribution. The nikcli bridge has explicit publication metadata. Use the <a href="/docs/api">HTTP reference</a> for integrations that should remain independent of internal package layout.</p>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
